This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \left(\Delta^2u\right)(x)=\lambda B(x)|u(x)|^{m-1}u(x)+|u(x)|^{2^* -1},
  \quad \mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u(x)|^2dx<+\infty,
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} where $4< m<4^*,$ $B(x)$ denotes the Kirchhoff-type biharmonic operator with the critical Sobolev exponent $4^*=\frac{4m}{4-2}$. Using the Mountain Pass theorem and variational technique, we establish the existence and asymptotic behavior of a normalized ground state solution for the given equation as the parameter $\lambda$ approaches zero or diverges. Additionally, we obtain an estimate for the normalized ground state solution when $\lambda$ is sufficiently small or large. This research extends and complements the related works in [21,22,24,26,27].\end{abstract}

